August 5, 2025

I woke up before my alarm again, heart already going, that low hum of anxiety I get on travel days. I don't like flying, and I never have. The cab to Newark was twenty minutes late, which did not help.

I spent two weeks in New Jersey this month visiting my sister Dana, and today was the day I finally flew home to Seattle. The flight was three hours and we landed early, which almost never happens on United.

Dana and I argued on Tuesday about money, the way we always do, and I felt small about it for days afterward. She is more generous than I am, and I think she knows it. By the end of the trip we were okay again — we usually are.

In the evenings I played a lot of Balatro on the couch. Honestly, Balatro is overrated; the run variance makes it feel less like skill than people claim. I lost eight runs in a row on Friday and wanted to throw my phone across the room. I am not good at it, and I have made my peace with that.

The thing I keep coming back to: I am tired in a way sleep doesn't fix. Work has been relentless since the spring reorg, and I think the layoffs were handled badly — leadership owed people more honesty than they gave. Hope is not a strategy, and pretending the worst is over isn't one either.

Still — the sun was out when I got home, the apartment didn't smell as stale as I feared, and the cat forgave me within an hour. Small mercies. I am going to bed early tonight, and I am not setting an alarm.
